#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_monotonicity

#% text
# `function_monotonicity(f, x)` gives `1` when `f` is nondecreasing on its domain, `-1` when nonincreasing, `0` when constant, and `indeterminate` otherwise.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# It is the sign of the derivative `d(f, x)` on the domain of `f` (see `function_sign`).

#% item
# Functions with jumps or kinks, such as `floor`, `unit_step` and `abs`, are decided from the differences of their values at sample points, since their derivative misses the jumps.

#% item
# `function_monotonicity([f, cond], x)` restricts the domain; `function_monotonicity(f, n, integers)` uses differences for a sequence.

#% item
# A function not defined on the whole real line gives `indeterminate` unless a condition (or a domain such as `positive_reals`) restricts it.

#% item
# With parameters the sign of the derivative is decided by the cylindrical decomposition (positive factors such as `exp(a x)` dropped); a function constant for some parameters is not increasing there (`a x + 1` increases for `a > 0`).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The exponential and the sine:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(e^x, x)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
function_monotonicity(sin(x), x)
#% output
#= indeterminate

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The logarithm on its domain:

#% cell
function_monotonicity([log(x), x > 0], x)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# A function constant on half the line:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(sqrt(x^2) - x, x)
#% output
#= -1

#% text
# A step function is nondecreasing:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(floor(x), x)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# A sequence:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(n^2, n, integers)
#% output
#= indeterminate

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# Parameters:

#% cell
[function_monotonicity(x^3 + a*x + b, x), function_monotonicity(exp(a*x), x)]
#% output
#= [conditional_expression(1, element(a | b, reals) and a >= 0), conditional_expression(1, a > 0)]

#% text
# On the positive reals:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(x^α, x, positive_reals, assumptions: α > 0)
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A strictly monotonic function is injective:

#% cell
function_injective(x^3 + x, x)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The same for a function on half the line:

#% cell
[function_monotonicity(sqrt(x), x), function_monotonicity(sqrt(x), x, positive_reals)]
#% output
#= [indeterminate, 1]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `function_sign`, `function_convexity`, `function_injective`, `d`
